  "worked_solution": "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/41f10e527834d0767709b965150054a564eaee9c174204381ed94b0e43077fe0.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/994e35e23e56191ef06a155912a326d28d857f236308655265d18d17bb48531b.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $x$ | $(2-y)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,3)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)>0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/9710358526e0e14fbf7263ba5d1496cdcb2941f9cb32a1d07b2ea37865163ab2.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
